    {
      "matchText": "Mediterranean trade",
      "term": "Mediterranean trade",
      "definition": "The web of seaborne commerce that tied the Roman world together, carrying African ivory, incense, gold, and gemstones north in exchange for Roman wine, olive oil, glassware, and coin. For Aksum it was the richest of its three trading horizons, with Roman Egypt as its biggest customer."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "African interior trade",
      "term": "African interior trade",
      "definition": "The overland caravan traffic that fed goods from deep inside the continent — ivory, gold dust, frankincense and myrrh, emeralds, hides, and enslaved people — to Aksum's highland markets and on to Adulis. Aksum rarely produced these goods itself; it controlled the point where they entered the international market and taxed the flow."
    },
